            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Elemental Abundance in the Earth's Crust</h3><p>The Earth's crust is composed of chemical elements in widely varying proportions. By mass percentage, the most abundant elements are:</p><ol><li><b>Oxygen ($O$)</b>: $\\approx 46.6\\%$ (most abundant element overall and non-metal)</li><li><b>Silicon ($Si$)</b>: $\\approx 27.7\\%$ (second most abundant element and metalloid)</li><li><b>Aluminum ($Al$)</b>: $\\approx 8.1\\%$ (<b>most abundant metal</b> in the crust)</li><li><b>Iron ($Fe$)</b>: $\\approx 5.0\\%$ (<b>second most abundant metal</b> in the crust; most abundant in Earth's core)</li><li><b>Calcium ($Ca$)</b>: $\\approx 3.6\\%$</li><li><b>Sodium ($Na$)</b>: $\\approx 2.8\\%$</li><li><b>Potassium ($K$)</b>: $\\approx 2.6\\%$</li><li><b>Magnesium ($Mg$)</b>: $\\approx 2.1\\%$</li></ol><h3>2. Minerals, Ores & Gangue</h3><ul><li><b>Mineral</b>: Naturally occurring inorganic homogeneous solid substance with a definite chemical composition and crystalline structure found in the crust.</li><li><b>Ore</b>: A mineral from which one or more metals can be extracted <b>conveniently and economically (profitably) on a commercial scale</b>. Hence: <i>'All ores are minerals, but all minerals are not ores.'</i> For example, Clay ($Al_2O_3 \\cdot 2SiO_2 \\cdot 2H_2O$) and Bauxite ($AlO_x(OH)_{3-2x}$) are both minerals of aluminum, but aluminum is economically extracted only from <b>Bauxite</b>.</li><li><b>Gangue / Matrix</b>: The undesirable earthly, sandy, or rocky impurities (silica, silicates, clay, limestone) physically associated with ore deposits in nature.</li><li><b>Native State</b>: Low-reactivity metals with highly positive reduction potentials that occur uncombined as free native elements in nature: <b>Gold ($Au$), Platinum ($Pt$), Silver ($Ag$)</b>, and occasionally Copper ($Cu$). Highly electropositive metals ($K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al$) are never found native; they occur solely in combined forms as oxides, sulfides, carbonates, or halides.</li></ul>",
