            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Principal Uranium Minerals & Deposits</h3><ul><li><b>Pitchblende (Uraninite)</b>: Complex uranium oxide ($U_3O_8$ or $UO_2 \\cdot 2UO_3$). Marie and Pierre Curie discovered <b>Radium</b> and <b>Polonium</b> by refining tons of pitchblende tailings.</li><li><b>Carnotite</b>: Hydrated potassium uranyl vanadate [$K_2(UO_2)_2(VO_4)_2 \\cdot 3H_2O$], a bright yellow secondary mineral.</li><li><b>Indian Uranium Deposits</b>: The premier operational underground uranium mines in India are located along the Singhbhum Thrust Belt in Jharkhand at <b>Jaduguda, Bhatin, Narwapahar, and Turamdih</b> (operated by Uranium Corporation of India Limited - UCIL). Major sandstone-type deposits are at Domiasiat (Meghalaya) and Tummalapalle (Andhra Pradesh, in Cuddapah basin).</li></ul><h3>2. Thorium Minerals & Monazite Beach Sands</h3><ul><li><b>Monazite</b>: A reddish-brown phosphate mineral containing rare-earth elements (Lanthanides) and significant quantities of <b>Thorium Oxide ($ThO_2$, $8-10.5\\%$)</b> and small amounts of Uranium ($0.3-0.4\\%$).</li><li><b>Geographic Distribution</b>: The world's largest placer deposits of monazite occur in the coastal beach sands of <b>Kerala (Chavara, Kollam), Tamil Nadu (Manavalakurichi), Odisha (Ganjam), and Andhra Pradesh</b>. Processed by Indian Rare Earths Limited (IREL).</li><li><b>Thorite</b>: Thorium silicate ($ThSiO_4$).</li></ul><h3>3. Strategic Significance: India's Three-Stage Nuclear Power Program</h3><p>Formulated by Dr. Homi J. Bhabha to exploit India's vast domestic Thorium reserves (~25% of world total) despite limited domestic Uranium:</p><ol><li><b>Stage I — Pressurized Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs)</b>: Uses natural Uranium-238 ($^{238}U$) with heavy water ($D_2O$) moderator to generate electricity and produce byproduct fissile <b>Plutonium-239 ($^{239}Pu$)</b>.</li><li><b>Stage II — Fast Breeder Reactors (FBRs)</b>: Uses $^{239}Pu$ fuel with a Thorium-232 ($^{232}Th$) blanket. Breeding produces more fuel than it consumes, converting fertile $^{232}Th$ into fissile <b>Uranium-233 ($^{233}U$)</b> (e.g., Kalpakkam PFBR).</li><li><b>Stage III — Thorium-Uranium-233 Breeders</b>: Self-sustaining reactors fueled by $^{233}U$ and $^{232}Th$, providing energy security for centuries.</li></ol>",
