            "title": "Beneficiation & Concentration: Gravity Separation, Froth Floatation & Leaching (Bayer's Process)",
            "tagline": "Crushing, pulverized ore, gravity separation (Wilfley tables, hydraulic washing), froth floatation for sulfide ores, collectors, frothers, depressants, and chemical leaching.",
            "readingTimeMins": 5,
            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Beneficiation (Concentration / Dressing) of Ore</h3><p>Mined ore contains vast quantities of unwanted gangue. The preliminary operation to remove rocky gangue and enrich the percentage of metal is <b>Concentration</b>:</p><ul><li><b>Hydraulic Washing / Gravity Separation</b>: Based on the difference in specific gravities between heavy ore particles and light gangue particles. Finely powdered ore is washed with an upward stream of running water on vibrating grooved <b>Wilfley tables</b>. Lighter gangue washes away; heavy ore settles. Used for heavy <b>oxide ores</b> (Haematite $Fe_2O_3$, Tinstone $SnO_2$) and native Gold.</li></ul><h3>2. Froth Floatation Process (Mrs. Carrie Everson / C.W. Green)</h3><p>Universally used for concentrating <b>Sulfide Ores</b> (Galena $PbS$, Zinc Blende $ZnS$, Copper Pyrites $CuFeS_2$). Based on the difference in <b>surface wetting properties</b>: sulfide ore particles are preferentially wetted by <b>oil</b>, while gangue particles are preferentially wetted by <b>water</b>.</p><ul><li><b>Frothers</b>: Pine oil, eucalyptus oil, or camphor (form stable froth).</li><li><b>Collectors</b>: Sodium/Potassium ethyl xanthate (adsorb on sulfide surface, rendering it water-repellent/hydrophobic).</li><li><b>Froth Stabilizers</b>: Cresol and Aniline.</li><li><b>Depressants</b>: Selectively prevent one sulfide from forming froth while allowing another. E.g., when an ore contains both $ZnS$ and $PbS$, <b>Sodium Cyanide ($NaCN$)</b> acts as a depressant. It forms a soluble coordination complex with $ZnS$ ($Na_2[Zn(CN)_4]$), preventing it from frothing, while $PbS$ rises into the froth.</li></ul><h3>3. Chemical Leaching & Bayer's Process</h3><p><b>Leaching</b> is the treatment of powdered ore with a suitable chemical reagent that selectively dissolves the desired metal into a soluble complex while leaving insoluble gangue behind.</p><ul><li><b>Bayer's Process for Bauxite</b>: Bauxite contains $Al_2O_3$ contaminated with $Fe_2O_3, TiO_2,$ and $SiO_2$. It is digested with concentrated $NaOH$ at $473 - 523\\text{ K}$ and $35 - 36\\text{ bar}$ pressure. Alumina dissolves as soluble sodium aluminate:$$Al_2O_3\\cdot 2H_2O + 2NaOH + 3H_2O \\rightarrow 2Na[Al(OH)_4]\\text{ (aq)}$$Insoluble $Fe_2O_3$ ('red mud') and $TiO_2$ are filtered out. Neutralizing with $CO_2$ gas precipitates pure hydrated alumina:$$2Na[Al(OH)_4] + CO_2 \\rightarrow Al_2O_3\\cdot 3H_2O\\downarrow + 2NaHCO_3$$Calcining at $1470\\text{ K}$ yields pure <b>Alumina ($Al_2O_3$)</b>.</li></ul>",
