            "title": "Blast Furnace Extraction of Iron: Zones, Fluxes (Limestone), Slag Formation & Pig Iron",
            "tagline": "Structure of blast furnace, charge (ore, coke, limestone), zones of combustion, reduction, slag formation, basic flux vs acidic gangue, Pig Iron vs Cast Iron.",
            "readingTimeMins": 5,
            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. The Blast Furnace Structure & Charge</h3><p>Iron is extracted from roasted haematite ($Fe_2O_3$) in a gigantic steel tower ($30\\text{ m}$ tall) lined with refractory fireclay bricks called a <b>Blast Furnace</b>. Pre-heated air blast ($600 - 800^\\circ\\text{C}$) is forced through nozzles called <b>tuyeres</b> near the base.</p><p>The furnace is fed continuously from the top through a double-cup-and-cone hopper with a <b>Charge ratio</b>: <b>Ore (Haematite, 8 parts) : Fuel (Coke, 4 parts) : Flux (Limestone $CaCO_3$, 1 part)</b>.</p><h3>2. Temperature Zones & Chemical Reactions</h3><ol><li><b>Zone of Combustion (Base, near Tuyeres, $1900 - 2200\\text{ K}$ / $1500 - 1900^\\circ\\text{C}$)</b>:<p>Coke burns in preheated air blast in a violent exothermic reaction:</p>$$C + O_2 \\rightarrow CO_2 + 393.5\\text{ kJ}$$Ascending $CO_2$ meets incandescent coke and is reduced endothermically to carbon monoxide:$$CO_2 + C \\rightarrow 2CO - 163.2\\text{ kJ}$$</li><li><b>Zone of Reduction (Upper Zone, $500 - 900\\text{ K}$ / $300 - 700^\\circ\\text{C}$)</b>:<p>Rising $CO$ gas reduces descending ferric oxide in sequential stages:</p>$$3Fe_2O_3 + CO \\rightarrow 2Fe_3O_4 + CO_2$$$$Fe_3O_4 + CO \\rightarrow 3FeO + CO_2$$$$FeO + CO \\rightarrow Fe\\text{ (spongy iron)} + CO_2$$</li><li><b>Zone of Slag Formation (Middle Zone, $1100 - 1300\\text{ K}$ / $800 - 1000^\\circ\\text{C}$)</b>:<p>Limestone decomposes into basic calcium oxide (flux):</p>$$CaCO_3 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} CaO + CO_2\\uparrow$$Basic $CaO$ reacts with acidic silicious gangue ($SiO_2$) to form molten <b>calcium silicate slag</b>:$$CaO\\text{ (basic flux)} + SiO_2\\text{ (acidic gangue)} \\rightarrow CaSiO_3\\text{ (molten slag)}$$Slag is less dense than molten iron and floats on top, preventing the hot iron from being re-oxidized by the air blast. Slag is tapped separately and sold to manufacture cement.</li><li><b>Zone of Fusion (Lower Zone, $1400 - 1700\\text{ K}$ / $1200 - 1400^\\circ\\text{C}$)</b>:<p>Spongy iron melts, dissolves $3-4\\%\\text{ C}$ along with small amounts of $Si, P, Mn,$ and $S$, and collects in the hearth.</p></li></ol><h3>3. Pig Iron vs Cast Iron vs Wrought Iron</h3><ul><li><b>Pig Iron</b>: The raw molten iron tapped directly from the blast furnace hearth, cast into crude ingots ('pigs'). Contains <b>$3.5 - 4.5\\%\\text{ Carbon}$</b> and impurities ($Si, Mn, P, S$). Extremely hard, brittle, low melting point (~1200°C), cannot be welded or forged.</li><li><b>Cast Iron</b>: Made by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke in a <b>Cupola furnace</b>. Contains <b>$2.5 - 3.5\\%\\text{ Carbon}$</b>. Slightly purer, expands slightly on solidification (reproduces mold details sharply), brittle. Used for sewer pipes, manhole covers, engine blocks, gutter grates.</li><li><b>Wrought Iron</b>: <b>Purest commercial form of iron</b> containing only <b>$0.1 - 0.25\\%\\text{ Carbon}$</b>. Made by puddling cast iron on a haematite hearth: $3Fe_3C + Fe_2O_3 \\rightarrow 11Fe + 3CO$. Highly malleable, ductile, tough, resistant to corrosion. Used for anchor chains, gates, railway couplings, bolts.</li></ul>",
