            "readingTimeMins": 5,
            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Nature of Steel & Modern Refining Goal</h3><p><b>Steel</b> is an alloy of iron and carbon containing between <b>$0.1\\%$ and $1.5\\%\\text{ Carbon}$</b>. To convert crude pig iron ($3.5-4.5\\%\\text{ C}$) into steel, the excess carbon, silicon, phosphorus, and sulfur must be oxidized and removed under strictly controlled conditions, followed by the addition of calculated quantities of carbon and alloying elements (Spiegeleisen: $Fe-Mn-C$ alloy).</p><h3>2. Historic & Modern Steelmaking Technologies</h3><ol><li><b>Sir Henry Bessemer's Converter (1856)</b>:<p>Pear-shaped tilting steel vessel lined with refractory bricks (acidic silica lining for low-P pig iron; basic dolomite [$MgCO_3\\cdot CaCO_3$] lining for high-P pig iron, termed the <b>Thomas-Gilchrist Process</b>). Air blasted from the bottom burns out $Si, Mn,$ and $C$. Highly exothermic, but difficult to control end-point quality.</p></li><li><b>Siemens-Martin Open-Hearth Process (1864)</b>:<p>Used regenerative thermal chambers; slower process allowing precise quality sampling, but energy-intensive and now obsolete.</p></li><li><b>Linz-Donawitz (LD) Process / Basic Oxygen Furnace (BOF)</b>:<p><b>Dominant modern steelmaking method (~70% of global steel)</b>. Developed in Linz and Donawitz, Austria (1952). Molten pig iron ($70-80\\%$) and steel scrap ($20-30\\%$) are charged into a basic-lined (magnesite/dolomite) converter. A water-cooled copper lance blows <b>pure supersonic oxygen ($>99.5\\%\\text{ O}_2$)</b> at $10-12\\text{ atm}$ onto the melt surface.</p><ul><li>Supersonic $O_2$ violently burns out carbon ($C + \\frac{1}{2}O_2 \\rightarrow CO\\uparrow$) and oxidizes $Si, Mn,$ and $P$ within just <b>40 minutes</b> for a $300\\text{-tonne}$ batch!</li><li>Basic flux ($CaO$) captures phosphorus: $6CaO + P_4O_{10} \\rightarrow 2Ca_3(PO_4)_2$ (<b>Thomas Slag</b>, ground and sold as phosphate fertilizer).</li></ul></li><li><b>Electric Arc Furnace (EAF) Steelmaking</b>:<p>Uses three massive graphite electrodes carrying tens of thousands of amperes to strike high-energy electric arcs ($>3500^\\circ\\text{C}$), melting <b>$100\\%$ recycled scrap steel</b> and Direct Reduced Iron (DRI / Sponge Iron). Highly flexible, green, and rapidly growing in mini-mills globally.</p></li></ol>",
