            "title": "Extraction of Zinc & Lead: Zinc Blende, Galena, Retort Reduction & Parkes Process",
            "tagline": "Roasting ZnS to ZnO, Belgian/Vertical Retort reduction, condensation of zinc vapor, Galena roasting, self-reduction of Pb, desilverization of lead via Parkes Process.",
            "readingTimeMins": 5,
            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Extraction of Zinc from Zinc Blende ($ZnS$)</h3><ol><li><b>Concentration</b>: Froth floatation using pine oil and xanthates.</li><li><b>Roasting</b>: Roasted in excess air at $900 - 1000^\\circ\\text{C}$:$$2ZnS + 3O_2 \\rightarrow 2ZnO + 2SO_2\\uparrow$$Sulfate formed is decomposed: $ZnSO_4 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} ZnO + SO_2 + \\frac{1}{2}O_2$.</li><li><b>Reduction (Belgian / Vertical Retort Process)</b>:<p>Roasted $ZnO$ is mixed with crushed Coke and heated in vertical refractory clay retorts to <b>$1400^\\circ\\text{C}$ ($1673\\text{ K}$)</b>:</p>$$ZnO + C \\rightarrow Zn\\text{ (vapor)} + CO\\uparrow$$<p><b>Zinc Distillation</b>: Because metallic zinc boils at <b>$907^\\circ\\text{C}$</b>, it vaporizes instantly inside the retort ($1400^\\circ\\text{C}$) and distills over. Vapors pass into an air-cooled condenser where liquid zinc condenses, termed <b>Spelter</b> (crude zinc, ~97-98% pure with $Pb, Fe, Cd$ impurities). Rapid chilling yields 'zinc dust'.</p></li></ol><h3>2. Extraction of Lead from Galena ($PbS$) & Parkes Process</h3><ol><li><b>Air Reduction (Auto-Reduction) Process</b>:<p>Galena is roasted in a reverberatory furnace with air to form $PbO$ and $PbSO_4$:</p>$$2PbS + 3O_2 \\rightarrow 2PbO + 2SO_2$$$$PbS + 2O_2 \\rightarrow PbSO_4$$<p>Air supply is then closed and temperature raised to trigger <b>Self-Reduction</b>:</p>$$2PbO + PbS \\rightarrow 3Pb\\text{ (molten)} + SO_2\\uparrow$$$$PbSO_4 + PbS \\rightarrow 2Pb + 2SO_2\\uparrow$$</li><li><b>Desilverization of Argentiferous Lead: The Parkes Process</b>:<p>Crude lead (Bullion) contains valuable dissolved <b>Silver ($Ag$)</b> ($0.01 - 1\\%$). Parkes process separates silver based on liquid-liquid extraction distribution coefficients:</p><ul><li>Molten lead bullion is mixed with $1 - 2\\%$ <b>Zinc metal ($Zn$)</b> at $\\approx 450^\\circ\\text{C}$.</li><li>(1) Zinc and Lead are mutually immiscible liquids (separate into two layers).</li><li>(2) Silver is <b>roughly 300 times more soluble in molten Zinc than in molten Lead</b> ($K_D \\approx 300$).</li><li>Silver migrates quantitatively into the upper, lighter zinc layer, forming an alloy ($Ag-Zn$) that floats on lead and solidifies as a top crust, skimmed off easily. Distilling off volatile zinc leaves pure silver behind!</li></ul></li></ol>",
