            "readingTimeMins": 5,
            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Extraction of Mercury from Cinnabar ($HgS$)</h3><p>Mercury has very low chemical affinity for oxygen (lies near the bottom of reactivity series, with positive standard reduction potential $E^\\circ_{Hg^{2+}/Hg} = +0.85\\text{ V}$). Consequently, <b>Mercuric Oxide ($HgO$) is thermally unstable</b> and decomposes spontaneously into liquid mercury and oxygen at temperatures above $400^\\circ\\text{C}$ ($2HgO \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2Hg + O_2$).</p><ul><li><b>Direct Roasting & Distillation</b>: Crushed cinnabar ($HgS$) is heated directly in a stream of air at $500 - 600^\\circ\\text{C}$:$$HgS + O_2 \\xrightarrow{500-600^\\circ\\text{C}} Hg\\text{ (vapor)} + SO_2\\uparrow$$Because mercury boils at <b>$356.7^\\circ\\text{C}$</b>, it immediately vaporizes along with $SO_2$ gas. The hot gases are drawn through water-cooled, serpentine cast-iron pipes where <b>mercury vapor condenses into liquid metal</b>, while $SO_2$ escapes to a scrubber. Crude liquid mercury is purified by filtering through chamois leather and washing with dilute $5\\%\\text{ HNO}_3$ to dissolve trace base metals ($Zn, Pb, Cu$).</li></ul><h3>2. MacArthur-Forrest Cyanide Process for Silver & Gold</h3><p>Invented in 1887 by John Stewart MacArthur and the Forrest brothers. Universally used for extracting <b>Silver ($Ag$)</b> and <b>Gold ($Au$)</b> from low-grade ores via hydrometallurgical leaching:</p><ol><li><b>Cyanidation / Leaching</b>:<p>Powdered silver ore (Argentite $Ag_2S$ or native Silver) is agitated with a dilute ($0.5\\%$) aqueous solution of <b>Sodium Cyanide ($NaCN$)</b> while a stream of compressed air ($O_2$) is vigorously bubbled through for 48 hours:</p>$$Ag_2S + 4NaCN \\rightleftharpoons 2Na[Ag(CN)_2]\\text{ (aq)} + Na_2S$$<p><b>Vital Role of Oxygen ($O_2$)</b>: Atmospheric oxygen oxidizes the byproduct sodium sulfide ($Na_2S$) into sodium thiosulfate and sulfate, driving the reversible equilibrium completely to the right:</p>$$2Na_2S + 2O_2 + H_2O \\rightarrow Na_2S_2O_3 + 2NaOH$$For native silver or gold, oxygen acts as the direct electron acceptor (oxidizing agent):$$4Au + 8NaCN + 2H_2O + O_2 \\rightarrow 4Na[Au(CN)_2]\\text{ (aq)} + 4NaOH$$</li><li><b>Precipitation / Displacement with Zinc Dust</b>:<p>The clear, filtered solution of soluble sodium dicyanoargentate(I) is treated with <b>Zinc dust</b>. Because Zinc is vastly more electropositive than Silver, it displaces silver from the cyanide complex:</p>$$2Na[Ag(CN)_2] + Zn \\rightarrow Na_2[Zn(CN)_4]\\text{ (aq)} + 2Ag\\downarrow\\text{ (black precipitate)}$$Precipitated silver is collected, fluxed with borax, and melted into solid silver bullion.</p></li></ol>",
