            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Principle of Vapor Phase Refining</h3><p>In <b>Vapor Phase Refining</b>, the crude metal is converted into a volatile chemical compound with a suitable reagent at a lower temperature, leaving non-volatile impurities behind. The volatile compound is then collected and heated to a higher temperature where it thermally decomposes back into <b>pure metal</b>, regenerating the reagent for reuse.</p><h3>2. Ludwig Mond's Carbonyl Process for Nickel (1890)</h3><p>Employed exclusively for the refining of <b>Nickel ($Ni$)</b>:</p><ol><li><b>Volatilization Stage (Low Temperature, $330 - 350\\text{ K}$ / $50 - 80^\\circ\\text{C}$)</b>:<p>Impure nickel is heated in a stream of <b>Carbon Monoxide ($CO$)</b> gas. Nickel reacts selectively to form volatile, toxic <b>Nickel Tetracarbonyl [$Ni(CO)_4$]</b>, leaving non-volatile impurities behind:</p>$$Ni\\text{ (impure)} + 4CO \\xrightarrow{330-350\\text{ K}} Ni(CO)_4\\text{ (volatile gas, BP } 42^\\circ\\text{C)}$$</li><li><b>Decomposition Stage (High Temperature, $450 - 470\\text{ K}$ / $180 - 200^\\circ\\text{C}$)</b>:<p>The gaseous $Ni(CO)_4$ is transferred into a decomposition chamber pre-heated to $180-200^\\circ\\text{C}$, where it thermally decomposes, depositing <b>ultra-pure Nickel ($>99.9\\%$)</b> onto pre-heated nickel pellets, while $CO$ gas is recycled:</p>$$Ni(CO)_4 \\xrightarrow{450-470\\text{ K}} Ni\\text{ (pure metal)} + 4CO\\uparrow$$</li></ol><h3>3. Anton Eduard van Arkel and Jan Hendrik de Boer Process (1925)</h3><p>Used for refining ultra-pure space-age and nuclear-grade metals: <b>Titanium ($Ti$), Zirconium ($Zr$), Hafnium ($Hf$), and Thorium ($Th$)</b>, particularly to remove embrittling trace interstitials (Oxygen and Nitrogen):</p><ol><li><b>Iodide Formation (Moderate Temperature, $500 - 850\\text{ K}$)</b>:<p>Impure metal is heated in an evacuated quartz reaction vessel with a small amount of <b>Iodine vapor ($I_2$)</b>. The metal forms a volatile covalent iodide, leaving refractory impurities behind:</p>$$Ti\\text{ (impure)} + 2I_2 \\xrightarrow{523\\text{ K}} TiI_4\\text{ (volatile vapor)}$$$$Zr\\text{ (impure)} + 2I_2 \\xrightarrow{870\\text{ K}} ZrI_4\\text{ (volatile vapor)}$$</li><li><b>Filament Decomposition (Extreme Temperature, $1700 - 2075\\text{ K}$)</b>:<p>The volatile iodide vapor diffuses toward an electrically heated <b>white-hot Tungsten filament</b> ($1700-2000\\text{ K}$) located inside the vessel. Upon striking the hot filament, the iodide decomposes: <b>pure metallic Titanium/Zirconium deposits on the tungsten wire</b>, growing into a thick crystal bar, while regenerated $I_2$ gas diffuses back to react with more crude metal (catalytic chemical transport cycle):</p>$$TiI_4 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Tungsten Filament, } 1700\\text{ K}} Ti\\text{ (ultra-pure)} + 2I_2\\uparrow$$$$ZrI_4 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Tungsten Filament, } 2075\\text{ K}} Zr\\text{ (ultra-pure)} + 2I_2\\uparrow$$</li></ol>",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Mond's Process refines Nickel via volatile nickel tetracarbonyl: $Ni + 4CO \\xrightarrow{50-80^\\circ\\text{C}} Ni(CO)_4 \\xrightarrow{180-200^\\circ\\text{C}} Ni + 4CO$.",
                "Van Arkel-de Boer method refines Titanium ($Ti$) and Zirconium ($Zr$) via volatile tetraiodides decomposed on a white-hot tungsten filament (~1700–2000 K).",
                "Van Arkel method strips out microscopic interstitial oxygen and nitrogen that cause severe embrittlement in space-age titanium alloys."
            ],
