            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Specialized Ferrous & Resistance Alloys</h3><p>Iron and nickel alloys form the backbone of modern structural, magnetic, and thermal engineering:</p><table border='1' cellpadding='4' style='border-collapse:collapse;'><tr><th>Alloy Name</th><th>Constituent Elements</th><th>Key Distinctive Property</th><th>Engineering / Household Applications</th></tr><tr><td><b>Stainless Steel (18/8)</b></td><td>$Fe\\text{ (73\\%)}, Cr\\text{ (18\\%)}, Ni\\text{ (8\\%)}, C\\text{ (0.1 - 0.2\\%)}$</td><td>Extreme corrosion resistance. Chromium forms a microscopic, self-healing <b>passive oxide film ($Cr_2O_3$, 2–3 nm thick)</b> that blocks oxygen and moisture from attacking underlying iron.</td><td>Kitchen cookware, surgical scalpels, food processing plants, chemical reactors, cutlery</td></tr><tr><td><b>Invar</b></td><td>$Fe\\text{ (64\\%)}, Ni\\text{ (36\\%)}$</td><td><b>Near-zero coefficient of thermal expansion</b> ($1.2 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ K}^{-1}$ between $-100^\\circ\\text{C}$ and $+100^\\circ\\text{C}$). Discovered by Charles Édouard Guillaume (1920 Nobel Prize).</td><td>Surveyor measuring tapes, pendulum clock rods, precision chronometers, seismic instruments, shadow masks</td></tr><tr><td><b>Alnico</b></td><td>$Al\\text{ (8 - 12\\%)}, Ni\\text{ (14 - 20\\%)}, Co\\text{ (20 - 24\\%)}, Fe\\text{ (balance)}$, with trace $Cu, Ti$</td><td><b>High magnetic coercivity and retentivity</b>; retains strong permanent ferromagnetism even at elevated temperatures ($>500^\\circ\\text{C}$).</td><td>Powerful permanent magnets, loudspeaker drivers, electric guitar pickups, electric motors, magnetrons</td></tr><tr><td><b>Nichrome</b></td><td>$Ni\\text{ (80\\%)}, Cr\\text{ (20\\%)}$</td><td><b>High electrical resistivity</b> and exceptional resistance to high-temperature oxidation due to adherent protective $Cr_2O_3$ film (operates safely up to $1150 - 1200^\\circ\\text{C}$ without burning).</td><td>Electric heating elements in household toasters, electric irons, geysers, hair dryers, industrial kilns</td></tr><tr><td><b>High-Speed Steel (HSS)</b></td><td>$Fe\\text{ (balance)}, W\\text{ (18\\%)}, Cr\\text{ (4\\%)}, V\\text{ (1\\%)}, C\\text{ (0.7\\%)}$ ('18-4-1' Steel)</td><td><b>Red Hardness</b>: Retains extreme cutting hardness even when heated to dull red heat ($>600^\\circ\\text{C}$) during high-speed machining.</td><td>Drill bits, lathe cutting tools, milling cutters, circular saw blades</td></tr><tr><td><b>Silicon Steel (Permalloy)</b></td><td>$Fe + 3-4\\%\\text{ Si}$</td><td>High magnetic permeability, low hysteresis loss and eddy current resistance.</td><td>Transformer cores, electric generator and motor laminations</td></tr><tr><td><b>Manganese Steel (Hadfield Steel)</b></td><td>$Fe + 12-14\\%\\text{ Mn}, 1\\%\\text{ C}$</td><td>Extreme impact toughness and work-hardening ability (gets harder the more it is struck).</td><td>Rock crushers, railway track switches/crossings, bulldozer blades, bank safe vaults</td></tr></table>",
