            "readingTimeMins": 5,
            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Lightweight Structural Alloys of Aluminum</h3><p>Pure aluminum is lightweight ($d \\approx 2.7\\text{ g/cm}^3$) but has low tensile strength (~90 MPa). Alloying creates high-strength aerospace and transport alloys:</p><ul><li><b>Duralumin</b>:<p>Composition: <b>$Al\\text{ (95\\%)}, Cu\\text{ (4\\%)}, Mg\\text{ (0.5\\%)}, Mn\\text{ (0.5\\%)}$</b>.</p><p><i>Age-Hardening (Precipitation Hardening)</i>: Invented by Alfred Wilm (1906). When quenched and aged at room temperature, sub-microscopic intermetallic precipitates ($CuAl_2$) disperse throughout the aluminum lattice, pinning dislocations. Tensile strength increases more than fourfold (up to $450\\text{ MPa}$), matching mild steel at one-third the weight!</p><p><i>Applications</i>: Aircraft fuselage frames, wings, Zeppelin airships, spacecraft structural trusses, high-speed racing bicycle frames.</p></li><li><b>Magnalium</b>:<p>Composition: <b>$Al\\text{ (90 - 95\\%)}, Mg\\text{ (5 - 10\\%)}$</b>.</p><p>Lighter than pure aluminum, highly ductile, easily machined, excellent corrosion resistance.</p><p><i>Applications</i>: Aircraft parts, scientific physical balances, lightweight precision optical instruments.</p></li></ul><h3>2. Low-Melting Alloys of Lead & Tin</h3><ul><li><b>Soft Solder</b>:<p>Composition: <b>$Pb\\text{ (37 - 50\\%)}, Sn\\text{ (50 - 63\\%)}$</b>.</p><p>The <b>Eutectic Solder ($63\\%\\text{ Sn} + 37\\%\\text{ Pb}$)</b> has a sharp, lowest melting point of <b>$183^\\circ\\text{C}$</b> (lower than pure Tin $232^\\circ\\text{C}$ and pure Lead $327^\\circ\\text{C}$), transitioning instantly from liquid to solid without a mushy phase. Used for joining electrical circuit wiring and copper plumbing.</p></li><li><b>Type Metal</b>:<p>Composition: <b>$Pb\\text{ (70 - 75\\%)}, Sb\\text{ (15 - 20\\%)}, Sn\\text{ (5 - 10\\%)}$</b>.</p><p><b>Key Physical Property</b>: Contains Antimony ($Sb$). Like bismuth and water, <b>Antimony expands upon solidification</b>. When cast into printing molds, type metal expands into every sharp groove of the matrix, producing crisp, flawless typographic letterforms for letterpress printing.</p></li><li><b>Rose's Metal & Wood's Metal (Fusible Alloys)</b>:<p><b>Wood's Metal</b>: $Bi\\text{ (50\\%)}, Pb\\text{ (25\\%)}, Sn\\text{ (12.5\\%)}, Cd\\text{ (12.5\\%)}$. Melts at an astonishingly low <b>$70^\\circ\\text{C}$</b> (below boiling water!). Used in automatic fire-sprinkler nozzle plugs, boiler safety thermal plugs, and electrical safety fuses.</p></li></ul>",
