            "readingTimeMins": 5,
            "theoryHtml": "<h3>1. Corrosion & Electrochemical Rusting of Iron</h3><p><b>Corrosion</b> is the slow, spontaneous degradation and deterioration of a metal into its oxide, sulfide, or carbonate through electrochemical reactions with its surrounding environment (moisture, atmospheric oxygen, and acidic pollutants like $CO_2, SO_2$).</p><p><b>Rusting of Iron</b> is an <b>electrochemical phenomenon</b> occurring via microscopic corrosion galvanic cells formed on the metal surface in the presence of water droplets containing dissolved $O_2$ and acidic $CO_2$ ($H_2CO_3$ electrolyte):</p><ul><li><b>Anode (Oxidation Site)</b>: Pure iron oxidizes, losing electrons into the metal bulk:$$2Fe\\text{ (s)} \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+}\\text{ (aq)} + 4e^- \\quad (E^\\circ_{Fe^{2+}/Fe} = -0.44\\text{ V})$$</li><li><b>Cathode (Reduction Site)</b>: Electrons travel through the metal to an impurity site or air-water interface where dissolved oxygen is reduced in acidic medium:$$O_2\\text{ (g)} + 4H^+\\text{ (aq)} + 4e^- \\rightarrow 2H_2O\\text{ (l)} \\quad (E^\\circ = +1.23\\text{ V})$$</li><li><b>Net Electrochemical Cell Reaction</b>:$$2Fe\\text{ (s)} + O_2\\text{ (g)} + 4H^+\\text{ (aq)} \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+}\\text{ (aq)} + 2H_2O\\text{ (l)} \\quad (E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = +1.67\\text{ V})$$</li><li><b>Rust Formation</b>: Ferrous ions ($Fe^{2+}$) migrate through the water droplet, further oxidized by atmospheric oxygen to ferric ions ($Fe^{3+}$), precipitating as <b>Hydrated Ferric Oxide (Rust)</b>:$$4Fe^{2+}\\text{ (aq)} + O_2\\text{ (g)} + 4H_2O + 2xH_2O \\rightarrow 2Fe_2O_3\\cdot xH_2O\\downarrow\\text{ (Rust)} + 8H^+$$</li><li><b>Destructive Flaking</b>: Unlike aluminum oxide, iron rust is porous, non-adherent, and expands by up to $600\\%$ in volume. It flakes off continuously, exposing fresh underlying iron to relentless corrosion until the structural member fails. Saline seawater accelerates rusting by acting as a high-conductivity electrolyte.</li></ul><h3>2. Advanced Corrosion Prevention Methods</h3><ol><li><b>Galvanization (Sacrificial Zinc Coating)</b>:<p>Coating iron or steel sheets with a thin protective layer of <b>Zinc ($Zn$)</b> by dipping into molten zinc at $450^\\circ\\text{C}$.</p><ul><li><b>Cathodic Protection Even When Scratched</b>: Zinc ($E^\\circ = -0.76\\text{ V}$) is more electropositive (more easily oxidized) than Iron ($E^\\circ = -0.44\\text{ V}$). If the zinc coating is scratched, exposed zinc acts as the sacrificial anode and corrodes preferentially ($Zn \\rightarrow Zn^{2+} + 2e^-$), supplying electrons to the iron, which remains protected as the cathode. (Conversely, <b>Tin-plated iron / Tin cans</b> protect iron only while intact; if scratched, iron corrodes <i>faster</i> because iron is more electropositive than tin $E^\\circ_{Sn^{2+}/Sn} = -0.14\\text{ V}$).</li></ul></li><li><b>Sacrificial Anodic Protection</b>:<p>Connecting underground steel pipelines, buried oil tanks, and ship hulls to sacrificial blocks of highly active electropositive metals—typically <b>Magnesium ($Mg$) or Zinc ($Zn$)</b>. The sacrificial block corrodes away and is replaced periodically, leaving the steel pipeline cathodically protected.</p></li><li><b>Impressed Current Cathodic Protection (ICCP)</b>:<p>Applying an external direct current (DC) power source that forces electrons onto the steel structure, making it a permanent cathode relative to inert auxiliary anodes (graphite or mixed metal oxide).</p></li><li><b>Barrier Protection</b>: Painting, greasing, enameling, and phosphating (treating with phosphoric acid to form protective iron phosphate).</li></ol>",
